/**
@mainpage RVCTuner

A vocal pitch editor. Open a take, and the melody in it is heard note by note, laid out on a
piano roll, and re-sung at whatever pitches you put the notes on. Nothing is sent to a server and
nothing runs live: the whole take is read once and rendered offline, a couple of seconds at a
time, so that an edit is audible before you have finished making it.

@section engines The two engines

Both engines take the same edited melody and differ only in what they need to know about the
voice.

rvctuner::MelSynthesiser runs **PC-NSF-HiFiGAN**, a vocoder conditioned on a mel spectrogram and
a fundamental separately. The mel carries the timbre and the words, the fundamental carries the
tune, and the network was trained on pairs where the two disagree. It therefore re-sings whoever
was recorded, with no model of them, which is what makes it the default for correcting a take.

rvctuner::VoiceSynthesiser runs an **RVC voice**: the content encoder strips the pitch out of the
recording and keeps the words and delivery, and the vocoder puts a melody back in. The vocoder's
weights are the voice, so this engine is a pitch corrector only when the voice it was trained on
is the voice in the recording. Voices are trained and exported with the sibling RTVoice project.

@section correcting What correction does

rvctuner::correctMelody splits each note into where it sat, how it wandered, how it shook, and
the detail below all three. Correction moves only where it sat. The wander and the shake are
scaled by whatever the note and the global dials ask for. The detail is always kept, because a
few hundredths of a semitone of unsteadiness is most of what separates a corrected note from a
synthesised one. The result is a shift per frame, smoothed across note boundaries alone, so that
tuning arrives and leaves the way a slide does rather than as a step.

@section layout Layout

Only the JUCE entry point sits at the top of `src/`; everything else is grouped by layer, and
includes are path-qualified so a file's dependencies name their layer.

| Directory | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `src/` | the application entry point |
| `src/common/` | rvctuner::BinaryMatrix |
| `src/dsp/` | rvctuner::SincResampler, rvctuner::ZeroPhaseFilter, rvctuner::MelSpectrogram, filter banks, smoothing, pitch maths |
| `src/model/` | rvctuner::PitchDetector and rvctuner::Synthesiser and their implementations, sessions, manifest |
| `src/edit/` | rvctuner::EditDocument, rvctuner::segmentNotes, rvctuner::correctMelody, rvctuner::RenderScheduler, rvctuner::Pipeline |
| `src/audio/` | rvctuner::TransportPlayer |
| `src/ui/` | rvctuner::MainComponent, rvctuner::EditorView, rvctuner::NoteGrid and the panels |

@section threads Which thread does what

The message thread owns the document and the editor and never blocks. rvctuner::Pipeline runs the
detector and reads the recording on its own thread, handing back the melody as soon as it is
heard so the editor is usable while the engine is still loading. rvctuner::RenderScheduler owns a
second thread and renders one span at a time, nearest the playhead first; the audio thread reads
finished spans through a sequence counter and falls back to the recording for anything not
rendered yet, so playback never waits.
*/
